    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d854">

                <pb facs="1580" n="1558" sameAs="frus:frus1951v02/pg1558"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">919.537/8–1951: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Panama</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Wiley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Panama City</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-08-19T14:00:00-05:00">August 19, 1951—2
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>152. FonMin and Pres both informed me noon yesterday with satisfaction and
                    evident pride proposed decree on Pan flagships had been redrafted (mytel 149,
                    Aug 18)<anchor n="1" xml:id="d854-f1"/> and that text completely consonant our
                    desires and with requirements situation.</p>
                <p>Last night Emb received copy in Spanish which forwarded Dept airmail today.
                    Although Pres does not expect sign decree until after seen by Asst Secy
                        Miller,<anchor n="2" xml:id="d854-f2"/> text was printed in full in local
                    morning papers.</p>
                <p>On first perusal Emb finds proposed Pan action comprehensive and as adequate as
                    could be hoped for.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d854-f3"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wiley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d854-f1" n="1" xml:id="d854-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d854-f2" n="2" xml:id="d854-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 160, August 21, 1951, Ambassador Wiley reported that President
                    Arosemena had actually signed the decree numbered 631 on August 18, and that his
                    signature had been kept secret temporarily in order to test public reaction
                    (919.537/8–2151).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d854-f3" n="3" xml:id="d854-f3-content">
                <p>In a memorandum dated January 8, 1952, Mr. Nufer stated in part that “the Decree
                    has been very effective in reducing the amount of Western flag shipping
                    available for the China trade. Panama’s cooperative effort, however, faces the
                    danger of nullification through re-registration of Panamanian vessels under
                    British and Portuguese flags”. (919.537/1–752)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
